{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"IMPLEMENTED AND PUSHED: Gerrit change 1157, commit on feat/llm-provider-seam.\n\nMEASURED: config.py 793 -> 631 (169 lines of headroom where there were 7); new\nsrc/rebar/llm/gate_context.py at 243 LOC, clearing the 100-line anti-fragmentation floor. That is\nroom for d23e's 10-16-line deprecation alias plus roughly fifteen more config knobs at the measured\n10-13 lines each.\n\nHELD-OUT TDD, and it held. I authored 8 tests, confirmed RED for the right reasons (module absent,\nconfig 793 > 700, warning attributed to rebar.llm.config, shared-bound loop present), then physically\nremoved the 5 held-out tests from the implementer's tree and handed it only the 3 happy-path cases.\nIt passed ALL FIVE it never saw — including the two that matter most: the re-export contract and the\nno-repoint guard.\n\nMUTATIONS, each restored afterwards:\n  * repoint a REAL consumer (attest.py) at gate_context      -> 1 test fails\n  * make gate_context import config at module scope (cycle)  -> 4 tests fail\n  * revert the logger to the hardcoded \"rebar.llm.config\"    -> 1 test fails\n\nTHE FIRST MUTATION IS THE WHOLE POINT and is worth spelling out, because the failure mode is silent\nrather than loud. Thirteen monkeypatch targets in the suite name `rebar.llm.config.<moved name>`.\nThey work because every consumer does a FUNCTION-LEVEL `from rebar.llm.config import ...`, resolving\nout of config's globals at call time — exactly what setattr rebinds. Repoint one consumer to\ngate_context and the patch still applies to config while the consumer reads elsewhere: THE PATCH\nSTOPS TAKING EFFECT AND ITS TEST KEEPS PASSING, asserting nothing. Nothing crashes; coverage silently\nevaporates. That is why this ships both a structural guard (no src module may import from\ngate_context) and a behavioural one (a patch on config must still reach a real consumer) rather than\ntrusting the relocation.\n\nCOORDINATION HAZARD RESOLVED IN THIS CHANGE. The d8ef seam test asserted workflow_ops.py and\nconfig.py against ONE shared bound. Tightening it for config.py would also have tightened it for\nworkflow_ops.py — still 794 until b5fe lands — so the test would have gone red naming the WRONG\nticket's file. Split into a per-file table: config.py at the 700 policy target, workflow_ops.py left\nat the staged cap. b5fe changes one number when it lands.\n\nHONEST LIMIT, stated in the new module's docstring as the plan requires: this RELOCATES INERT MASS\nand does NOT remove the absorber. `from_env` still costs 10-13 lines per new knob, and the domain\nmoved out was chosen partly because it is inert (no growth since 2026-07-09). Roughly fifteen knobs\nof runway, not permanence. The durable follow-ups are (a) teach gen_env_registry.py to consume an\nexported spec list and only THEN table-ify from_env, or (b) domain-split the knobs per feature\ncluster. ADR 0056's declarative-field-table suggestion remains NOT viable as written: the generator\nrecords a read only when the env name is a STRING LITERAL, so a spec-row table makes 19 rows vanish\nfrom docs/env-vars.md and fails the CI drift gate.\n\nGATES: make lint clean (after rewrapping over-long docstring prose — ruff format does not reflow\ndocstrings), make typecheck clean, docs/env-vars.md regenerated for the moved\nREBAR_GATE_ALLOW_UNGATED row. A broad tests/unit + tests/interfaces run is in progress as independent\nno-regression evidence.\n\nNOT CLOSING until 1157 is Verified +1."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785577105790144001,"uuid":"fe968129-ee78-4d55-95aa-bd7f64ae4c47"}